  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nVisible satellite and surface observations show a persistent\nstratocumulus deck around 3000 to 5000 ft wrapping southward\nacross northern West Virginia and the Northeast Mountains,\ndriven by broad cyclonic flow around the departing low pressure\nsystem off the New England coast. Regional soundings, including\nthe 12Z PIT observation, depict moist low-level profiles with\nsaturation banked beneath a subsidence inversion around H850.\nWeak orographic ascent will continue to squeeze out isolated\nlight sprinkles or drizzle along the western windward slopes\nthrough early evening. Across the western valleys, southeast\nOhio, and northeast Kentucky, skies have remained mostly sunny,\nallowing temperatures to rise into the lower 70s. \n\nCentral guidance dew points have been adjusted downward 3 to 6 \ndegrees for this afternoon and evening to correct for typical \nhigh bias in dry advection regimes. For tonight, light northerly\nwinds will decouple quickly as surface ridging noses in from \nthe northwest. In areas across the western valleys and southern \ncoalfields that remain largely clear, efficient radiational \ncooling will trigger rapid river valley fog development late \nthis evening, becoming locally dense after midnight. Farther \nnortheast soundings suggest boundary layer RH will remain near \nsaturation; thus, any breaks in the cloud canopy overnight will \nlead to almost immediate, dense surface fog and stratus \ndevelopment. Any fog should dissipate through 10-11 am Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nBroad surface high pressure shifts across the central\nAppalachians on Tuesday, keeping boundary layer flow light and\ngenerally easterly. As the coastal circulation lifts\nnortheastward toward the Canadian Maritimes, an upper-level\nridge builds eastward across the Ohio and Tennessee Valleys\nthrough midweek. \n\nLow-level flow transitions southwesterly by Wednesday. Lowland \nhigh temperatures will climb from the mid 70s on Tuesday into \nthe lower to mid 80s Wednesday and Thursday. Forecast profiles \nindicate deep vertical mixing tapping into very dry air between \nH850 and H700, which will mix afternoon surface relative \nhumidities down into the 30 to 40 percent range. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nGlobal ensembles continue to resolve split-stream flow late in\nthe week, with an active subtropical jet carrying deep moisture\ndownstream from the remnants of Eastern Pacific Tropical Cyclone\nPolo into the southern Plains and Lower Mississippi Valley.\nWhile some deterministic guidance and central guidance show \nslight chances of showers edging into western zones late \nThursday night into Friday. 10th percentile QPF shows \nnegligible accumulation prior to Friday night as the surface \ncold front slowly settles southward. \n\nAtmospheric moisture surges over the weekend with precipitable\nwater values climbing toward 1.7 inches in weak steering flow \naloft. However, heavy rainfall and flash flood threats appear \nminimal across the local area. Severe storm and intense rainfall\nrates will be heavily mitigated by meager instability, as mid- \nlevel lapse rates remain poor (5.0 to 5.5 C/km) due to \nwidespread upstream convection over the southern Plains \nintercepting the thermodynamic plume. Furthermore, vegetation is\nstill quite active for early October given recent warm and wet \nconditions and we will have had nearly a week without meaningful \nprecipitation for soils to recover.",
